<title>USA: USDA cotton data seen neutral to barely bullish.</title>
<headline>USDA cotton data seen neutral to barely bullish.</headline>
<dateline>NEW YORK 1997-02-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Cotton futures prices are not expected to react dramatically to USDA's February crop report, market sources said.</p>
<p>USDA put exports at 6.5 million 480-lb bales from 6.3 million bales last month. Output was seen unchanged at 18.95 million bales.</p>
<p>&quot;There is no surprise there,&quot; Merrill Lynch analyst Judith Ganes, said.</p>
<p>Sharon Johnson, an analyst with Frank Schneider and Co Inc, said the report could lift prices about 0.20 cent a lb on the open.</p>
<p>She cited the export number, a 200,000-bale increae in China's imports to 1.90 million bales, and a 200,000-bale increase in U.S. consumption to 11.2 million bales, as mildly bullish.</p>
<p>While ending stocks fell to 4.45 million bales from 4.70 million bales, &quot;we still have lots of cotton,&quot; Johnson said.</p>
<p>((Suzanne Rostler, New York Commodities, 212-859-1640))</p>
